Implemented and locally verified the Track B generator based on Proposition 5.10’s native permutation construction from the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.13392).

Key results:

- All nine local gates pass.
- Planted witnesses: 16/16 verified.
- Guessing: 0/250,000.
- Four attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Reference algorithm: 8/8, averaging 1,190,367 operations and 0.283 seconds.
- Compact structural route: 233 operations.
- Canonical-key relabelling tests: 20/20.
- No graph reduction is used.

Files:

- [gen_2412_13392.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.13392/gen_2412_13392.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.13392/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.13392/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.13392/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.13392/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2412.13392/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

External hardening remains blocked: every OpenRouter request returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded,” leaving zero scored oracle attempts. Consequently, `submit.sh` correctly refuses release. This is an external account blocker, not a G/H/V rejection; no `REJECTED.md` was created.
